<p><strong>Product</strong>: <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/projects/nvalt/" target="_blank">nvALT</a><br>
<strong>Price</strong>: Free</p>
<p>Christmas came early this year. <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/" target="_blank">Brett Terpstra</a> released a new version of nvALT. Compatible with modern versions of macOS, it left me dancing with joy.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_c4a729f3205a44e5a9669523ddfb8b2e/_image_cache/d4d8d15a-e151-4a7f-bc21-2b1624110744/nvalt.png" alt="nvALT" width="3000" height="2126" data-action="zoom"><span class="caption">nvALT</span></p>
<h2 id="simple">Simple</h2>
<p>Before Obsidian, and its ilk, nvALT was the notes app I lived in. It was distinguished by its simplicity. You typed in the unibar to both search your notes and create new notes. I was in plain text mode and it was fantastic.</p>
<p>Over time, other programs came along and I moved away from it. I remember that I loved the program for how simple it was. No friction. Just notes. Easy creation and efficient search.</p>
<h2 id="revival">Revival</h2>
<p><a href="https://brettterpstra.com/" target="_blank">Brett Terpstra</a> has revived nvALT to be compatible with modern versions of macOS. He has been active in the development of nvALT in the last few weeks. I am not going to try to explain the features of the product. It is something you need to discover on your own. I am going to instead talk about simplicity and its alternative.</p>
<h2 id="complexity">Complexity</h2>
<p>I used to love <a href="https://obsidian.md/" target="_blank">Obsidian</a>. It is a great product. It is extended through community plugins. It does a whole lot of stuff. You need to live in it for a while to be comfortable in it. There is nothing wrong with the program. It is capable, full-featured and does a whole host of things. There are people living in it and enjoying the experience. It is not for me. Firstly, it is Electron. So it eats memory. It is responsive, that is not the problem. It just takes too much memory. Secondly, it is by design something that is a distracting mess. Let me explore this idea in more detail.</p>
<p>I use <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/" target="_blank">Emacs</a>, I live in it. I use it for everything I can. It has the steepest damn learning curve that I have ever tackled in a program. However I am comfortable with it. Took me a long time to get here. But I am happy now. I don’t make too many changes to the setup. I have it working, I am happy with it, there is no reason to monkey with something which works. Every bit of the configuration is something I understand and know. There is comfort in that. If I feel the need to change something, I can. It is not a burden anymore. I have learned the keyboard commands I need. I live in it.</p>
<p>I never got that feeling in Obsidian. It was distracting. I spent an inordinate amount of time playing with plugins. Trying to customize keyboard commands I wanted. Trying to figure out bases and canvas and somewhere in the middle of using it, it occurred to me that I was not taking notes but tweaking the system. That got old really quick. I stopped using it and moved everything into Emacs.</p>
<p>Don’t get me wrong. I spent a lot of time doing the same thing in Emacs. I am a tweaker. I try to tweak everything and Emacs is heaven when it comes to the ability to customize an editor. Somewhere in that exercise I got familiar with the fundamentals of the program. It was not a strain anymore. My familiarity with it changed how I reacted to it. I get my work done in it. It doesn’t get in the way anymore.</p>
<p>If I did the same kind of immersion into Obsidian, I would be as comfortable in it as I am today in Emacs. I could not see beyond the memory usage and moved on from Obsidian.</p>
<h2 id="simplicity">Simplicity</h2>
<p>Back to the notion of simplicity. nvALT is simple. You set up the preferences once and then you are ready to go. There are keyboard commands. You can merge them to your muscle memory with continued usage.</p>
<p>There is no cognitive overload.</p>
<p>Conceptually I look at cognitive overload in two ways:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li>The cognitive effort which is required to use the program. The features, the keyboard commands, the nuances of the interface, and so on. It is the effort which is directly related to the use of the program.</li>
<li>The second part of the effort is the actual note-taking. What am I writing? How do I say what I want to say?</li>
</ol>
<p>nvALT reduces the first part and that is the intrinsic feature of this program.</p>
<p>Creating and searching notes is simple. You don’t spend any energy on the program. You can concentrate on your notes/writing. It is liberating. It just works.</p>
<p>What are the downsides of this simplicity? Goes against all the models of knowledge management. You can link between documents. The old-style wikilinks work well. There are no back links. There are no useless graphs of your knowledge base. You are not going to have a canvas. You are not going to have database functions. No queries to manage. It is a bunch of text files in a folder and that is about it.</p>
<h2 id="other-solutions">Other solutions</h2>
<p>nvALT is not the only program which provides these features. <a href="https://zettelkasten.de/the-archive/" target="_blank">The Archive</a> is a paid solution ($19), which provides similar features and is great.</p>
<p><a href="https://fsnot.es/" target="_blank">FSNotes</a> is a free alternative which does more than both nvALT and The Archive. It lets you manage multiple folders and it does a great job of that.</p>
<h2 id="the-elephant-in-the-room">The Elephant in the Room</h2>
<p>Brett Terpstra and Fletcher T. Penney have a program which has been in beta for a long time. The product is called <a href="https://nvultra.com/" target="_blank">nvUltra</a>, if and when it gets released, it is going to be a major competitor in this space. I have been on the beta from the start and love the program.</p>
<h2 id="the-conclusion">The Conclusion</h2>
<p>While I wait for nvUltra to be released, nvALT is a great resource which I am happy to be using after a long time. Download the program and use it for a while, you might also discover the charms of simplicity when it comes to your note-taking.</p>
<p>Recommended heartily.</p>
